  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nPrecip chances... Radar continues to show weak returns percolating\nover the forecast area late this evening as transient shortwave \nenergy passes over the area. However, absence of low level \nforcing and dry pocket between around 850 and 650 mb should keep \nQPF amounts light. Prominent subsidence inversion will then keep \nlow stratus around through most of the afternoon on Wednesday.\n\nEnsembles continue to hone in on phasing system Thursday into \nFriday, with guidance showing northern and southern stream lows\nmerging over the Great Lakes Thursday night into Friday morning.\nModels generate light rain over central Wisconsin beginning early\nTuesday afternoon as northward moisture transport brings PWATs\nto near 90th percentile (1.25 to 1.5\"), with rain coverage\nspanning the entire forecast area by late in the afternoon. Signs\npoint toward a soaking rainfall for most of northeast Wisconsin,\nwith NBM probabilities showing a 40 to 70% signal for 0.5\" of QPF\nalong/south of the Hwy 29 corridor. Wrap around showers then \ncontinue through Friday, though how long they do will likely \ndepend on whether phasing occurs directly overhead or further \neast. Latest suite of model runs have backed off on light snow\npotential across the far north on Friday, though a few flakes \nmay mix in at times as 850 mb temps fall below 0C and robust\nnorthwest flow kicks in.\n\nWinds... Pressure gradient tightens up along the back edge of the\ndeparting surface low Thursday night into Friday, putting us \nunder robust northwest flow regime. Combination of deep mixing and\nstrong CAA will allow for transport of near 50 knot winds at 850\nmb down to the surface, resulting in potential (30 to 60%) for \ngusts to 40 mph across the forecast area, highest along the\nlakeshore. As such, pretty slam dunk that marine hazards will be\nneeded during this time, as NBM shows healthy signal (60 to 90%) \nfor gale force gusts over all marine zones.\n\nTemperatures... Arctic high slides south Friday into Saturday, \nbringing a brief spell of well below normal temperatures and \nincreased potential for frost/freeze. As highs struggle to make it\ninto the 50s for some areas Friday afternoon, Friday night lows \nare forecast to plummet into the mid 20s to low 30s inland of Lake\nMichigan as skies clear out and winds decouple. As such, expect \nthat frost/freeze headlines will be needed in the coming days. \nReturn flow then quickly sets up over the weekend, resulting in \nhigh temperatures rebounding into the 60s.",
